"Connects two comparable values within each category so direction and absolute change are visible.\n\nUse when: The same entities have exactly two measurements on a shared scale. Both endpoint levels and their differences matter.\n\nAvoid when: Unmatched groups or many repeated time points would imply false pairing.\n\nVisual grammar: Two dot positions encode the paired values. A thin segment encodes their numeric separation within each category row.\n\nBuild: Join measurements using stable entity identifiers and disclose missing pairs. Place each pair on one row using one quantitative scale. Use consistent endpoint symbols and label unusually large changes outside marks.\n\nCheck: Check endpoint order represents the stated before/after relationship. Verify connector lengths equal absolute value differences. Confirm paired observations use compatible definitions and denominators.\n\nPitfalls: Segments must remain within their own rows and away from labels. Overlapping endpoints can hide zero change; use a distinct zero-change symbol.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic delivery times before and after a routing update, one paired row per depot.\n\nAlternatives: Slope chart, Dot plot, Range bar chart."
